# Lesson 5, new exercise: after 2♠, responder invites with a fit; opener declines.
title: A spade fit, an invitation
id: L05-21
type: exercise
lesson: 5
concepts: 5.5
student: S
board: 44
dealer: W
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  A976   T86    AQ8    KQ4
  E  853    A42    942    AJ97
  S  QJ42   Q5     K76    8532
  W  KT     KJ973  JT53   T6

auction:
  W Pass
  N 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
  E Pass
  S 2C*    = Stayman: do you have a 4-card major?
     key: 8 points and four spades: Stayman.
     not 2NT: You have four spades. Ask first.
  W Pass
  N 2S     = Four spades, not four hearts
  E Pass
  S 3S*    = A spade fit, 8-9 points: an invitation
     key: You've found a fit, and with 8 points you invite with 3♠.
     not 4S: With 8 points, invite and let partner decide.
     not 2NT: You've found a spade fit. Raise spades.
  W Pass
  N Pass   = A minimum: decline
  E Pass

auction recap:
  Partner showed four spades, so you had a fit. With 8 points you invited with
  3♠, and partner, with a minimum 15, declined. North is declarer, because
  North bid spades first, and so East will lead to the first trick.

play:
  1   E D9  S D6  W D3  N DQ
  2   N DA  E D2  S D7  W D5
      note: Cash the ♦A.
  3   N HT  E H2  S H5  W HJ
      note: Lead hearts to ruff one in dummy, the short trump hand.
  4   W HK  N H6  E H4  S HQ
  5   W H9  N H8  E HA  S S2
      note: East plays the ace, and dummy ruffs.
  6   S SQ  W ST  N S6  E S3
      note: Draw trumps.
  7   S SJ  W SK  N SA  E S5
      note: Keep drawing trumps.
  8   N S9  E S8  S S4  W H3
      note: Trumps are drawn.
  9   N CK  E C7  S C2  W C6
      note: Lead clubs to knock out the ace.
  10  N CQ  E CA  S C3  W CT
      note: Keep pushing clubs.
  11  E CJ  S C5  W H7  N C4
  12  E C9  S C8  W DT  N S7
      note: Declarer ruffs.
  13  N D8  E D4  S DK  W DJ
      note: 9 tricks: 3♠ made.

play recap:
  Declarer's plan: draw trumps, use dummy's trumps to ruff a heart, and give
  up the tricks that can't be avoided.

  3♠ made exactly, and 4♠ would have gone down. Inviting, rather than
  bidding game yourself, let partner judge with the full picture, and his
  minimum said stop.
